{
 "thread": "TcLKIMy1Qvs",
 "subject": "jointly and freely estimating in multiple groups that can overlap",
 "messages_listed": 5,
 "last_activity": "2018-04-12T11:30:44Z",
 "messages": [
  {
   "id": "gIl5yXszEAAJ",
   "from": "Drew Altschul",
   "author": "19db333399a1b397",
   "date": "2017-02-13T11:05:01Z",
   "subject": "jointly and freely estimating in multiple groups that can overlap",
   "parts": [
    [
     1,
     "<div dir=\"ltr\">Hi everyone,<br><br>I have a question about constraining paths with groups, in what<br>\r\nappears to be a slightly unusual way.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nI have a fixed-weighted-sum-scored &quot;latent variable&quot;, and several<br>\r\nobserved variables I want to regress onto the LV. I also have three<br>\r\ngroups, which can be organized as 2 groups in 2 different ways (1<br>\r\ngroup overlaps with both other groups, but via different attributes<br>\r\nfor each other group). The model definition is quite simple:<br><br><div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">LV </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a4 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#066\">1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">*</span><span style=\"color:#000\">a5<br><br>LV </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c1 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c2 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c3 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c4 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c5 </span><span style=\"color:#660\">+</span><span style=\"color:#000\">c6</span></div></code></div>\r\n<br>\r\nSome of these variables are &quot;of interest&quot; - the c vars - and some are not - the b vars. The<br>\r\nnot-interesting variables I want to include as controls, and for these<br>\r\nI want to allow their regression estimates onto the LV to be freely<br>\r\nestimated in all models - e.g., b1 ought to have a different<br>\r\nregression coefficient for each group.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nAt the same time, I want to be able to be able to have the of-interest c<br>\r\nregression coefficients jointly estimated, but not always jointly<br>\r\nestimated across all groups.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nThe syntax for estimating all three groups separately is simple enough:<br>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">lavaan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">type</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;sem&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3samples</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div>Specify the groups, but don&#39;t specify anything to be equal.<br>\r\n<br>\r\nJointly estimating all parameters of-interest across the entire<br>\r\ndataset is easy too:<br>\r\n<div style=\"background-color:rgb(250,250,250);border-color:rgb(187,187,187);border-style:solid;border-width:1px;word-wrap:break-word\"><code><div><span style=\"color:#000\">lavaan</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\">data</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> model</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">type</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&#39;sem&#39;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#066\">3samples</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#000\">equal </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#080\">&quot;regressions&quot;</span><span style=\"color:#660\">),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#008\">group</span><span style=\"color:#660\">.</span><span style=\"color:#008\">partial</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">=</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> c</span><span style=\"color:#660\">(</span><span style=\"color:#000\">LV </span><span style=\"color:#660\">~</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> b1</span><span style=\"color:#660\">,</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...),</span><span style=\"color:#000\"> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">...</span><span style=\"color:#000\"><br> </span><span style=\"color:#660\">)</span></div></code></div><br>\r\nBut if I want to jointly estimate coefficients for the c vars in 2 of the groups, and<br>\r\nestimate different c coefficients for the other group, while allowing<br>\r\nthe b vars to be freely estimated ... Well, I have no idea how<br>\r\nto do this and I haven&#39;t been able to find anything while scouring the <br>web, either. I *think* this should at very least be doable<br>by specifying everything within the definition of the models,<br>\r\nbut again, I don&#39;t know the syntax. Does anyone know how I should<br>go about this?<br><br>Many thanks,<br>                Drew<br><br></div>"
    ]
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "47BaxwqfEAAJ",
